{"name":"Lords of the Night","hex":"#664488","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"lords","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of lord"]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["third-person singular simple present indicative of lord"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lords"},{"term":"night","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The time when the Sun is below the horizon when the sky is dark.","The period of darkness beginning at the end of evening astronomical twilight when the sun is 18 degrees below the horizon, and ending at the beginning of morning astronomical twilight."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Ellipsis of good night."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/night"}],"entries":[{"title":"Lords of the Night","blocks":[{"text":"In Mesoamerican mythology the Lords of the Night are a set of nine deities who each ruled over every ninth night forming a calendrical cycle. Each lord was associated with a particular fortune, bad or good, that was an omen for the night that they ruled over."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Codex_Borgia_page_14.jpg/330px-Codex_Borgia_page_14.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lords_of_the_Night","wikidata":"Q1640892"},{"title":"Lords of the Night (Venice)","blocks":[{"text":"The Lords of the Night were magistrates in the Republic of Venice that operated in special courts."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/The_Lords_of_the_Night.jpg/330px-The_Lords_of_the_Night.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lords_of_the_Night_(Venice)","wikidata":"Q3960407"},{"title":"Night","blocks":[{"text":"Night, or nighttime, is the period of darkness when the Sun is below the horizon. Daylight illuminates one side of Earth, leaving the other in darkness. The opposite of nighttime is daytime. Earth's rotation causes the appearance of sunrise and sunset. Moonlight, airglow, starlight, and light pollution dimly illuminate night. The duration of day, night, and twilight varies depending on the time of year and the observer's latitude."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg/330px-Savault_Chapel_Under_Milky_Way_BLS.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Night","wikidata":"Q575"},{"title":"Midnight blue","blocks":[{"text":"Midnight blue is a dark shade of blue named for its resemblance to the apparently blue color of a moonlit night sky around a full moon. Midnight blue is identifiably blue to the eye in sunlight or full-spectrum light, but can appear black under certain more limited spectra sometimes found in artificial lighting. It is similar to navy, which is also a dark blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg/330px-D%C3%BCsseldorf_Midnight_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Midnight_blue","wikidata":"Q947638"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Gaetano_Donizetti_-_Marino_Faliero_-_tragedia_lirica_in_tre_atti_da_rappresentarsi_nell%27I._R._Teatro_alla_Scala_la_primavera_1840_-_libretto_%28IA_marinofalierotra00bide%29.pdf/page38-500px-thumbnail.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Gaetano_Donizetti_-_Marino_Faliero_-_tragedia_lirica_in_tre_atti_da_rappresentarsi_nell%27I._R._Teatro_alla_Scala_la_primavera_1840_-_libretto_%28IA_marinofalierotra00bide%29.pdf/page38-500px-thumbnail.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Scene from the 1840 opera Marino Falie